## Why `transform` and `opacity` Animations Are Smooth (and `top`/`left` Aren't)

### The expensive way: animating `left`, `top`, `width`, `height`
When you animate something's position using `left` or `top` (or resize it with `width`/`height`), you're actually changing that element's real position/size in the document. This can affect other elements around it too — so the browser has no choice but to run the **full pipeline**: Layout (recalculate position), Paint (redraw the pixels), and Composite. And since animations typically update **60 times per second**, that's the full expensive pipeline running 60 times every second.

### The cheap way: animating `opacity`
Changing `opacity` doesn't move anything, doesn't resize anything, and doesn't change what's actually drawn — it only changes **how transparent the already-drawn picture looks**. So the browser can skip Layout AND Paint entirely, and just tell the Composite stage "show this same picture, but more/less see-through."

### The cheap way: animating `transform` (translate/scale/rotate)
This one's a bit sneaky — visually, it *looks* like the element moved or resized, but as far as the rest of the page is concerned, its actual layout box never changed. It's like picking up an already-printed photo and physically sliding it across a table — you're not redrawing the photo, just moving where it sits. So the browser can (usually) skip Layout AND Paint here too, and just tell Composite "place this same picture somewhere else / at a different size," which the GPU can do extremely fast.

### Why this makes the animation feel smoother, even if your JS is busy
Composite runs on its **own separate thread** — completely independent from the main thread that runs your JavaScript. So a `transform`/`opacity` animation keeps playing smoothly **even if your JavaScript is busy** doing something else (handling a click, running a heavy function) — it's not stuck waiting in line behind your other code.

### The catch — it's not automatically free
To get this speed benefit for `transform`, the browser generally needs to first "promote" that element onto its **own separate layer** (like cutting it out and putting it on its own transparent sheet of glass, so moving it doesn't require redrawing anything else). Browsers often do this automatically, but you can also explicitly hint it with:
```css
will-change: transform;
```
**Important:** don't sprinkle this everywhere by default. Every promoted layer costs GPU memory, and creating too many of them ("layer explosion") can actually make performance *worse*, not better. Use it deliberately — right before an animation happens, and consider removing it once the animation is done.

