## Quick-Fire Q&A (Good for Rapid Recall)

**Q: Besides `offsetHeight`, what else forces a synchronous layout?**
A: `offsetWidth`, `offsetTop`, `offsetLeft`, `clientWidth`/`clientHeight`, `scrollWidth`/`scrollHeight`, `scrollTop`/`scrollLeft` (after a mutation), `getBoundingClientRect()`, and `getComputedStyle()` (for layout-affecting properties).

**Q: Does `opacity: 0` remove an element the same way `display: none` does?**
A: No. `opacity: 0` still takes up its space in the layout and is still technically "painted" — it's just fully see-through. `display: none` actually removes the element from layout completely. `visibility: hidden` is in between — keeps its layout space, but skips drawing its visible content.

**Q: How would you actually find layout thrashing in a real app?**
A: Record a session in Chrome DevTools' Performance panel and look for repeated "Recalculate Style"/"Layout" pairs happening rapidly — DevTools even explicitly flags these as "Forced reflow" warnings when it detects this pattern.

**Q: Why can `opacity` skip Paint, but changing `background-color` can't?**
A: `opacity` just changes how an already-drawn picture is blended onto the screen — the actual pixels never change. `background-color` changes the actual pixel content itself, so the browser has to redraw (repaint) that area to reflect the new color.

**Q: What's the downside of adding `will-change: transform` to every animated element by default?**
A: Each one becomes its own separate layer, using GPU memory. Too many layers can overwhelm the GPU/compositor and actually make things slower — this is called "layer explosion."

