## The Interview-Ready Answer (Say This Out Loud)

> "The browser turns a page into pixels through four stages: Style, where it resolves CSS into computed values; Layout, where it calculates the actual size and position of every element; Paint, where it rasterizes those into pixels; and Composite, where a separate thread assembles everything onto the screen, often using the GPU. Layout thrashing happens when JavaScript writes something that invalidates layout — like changing width — and then immediately reads a layout-dependent value like offsetHeight, forcing the browser to synchronously recalculate layout right then instead of waiting to batch it. Doing that in a loop means layout recalculates on every single iteration instead of once — that's the jank. The fix is separating all your reads from all your writes — measure everything first, then apply changes after. Transform and opacity animations avoid this whole problem because neither one changes an element's actual layout geometry or, if it's on its own composited layer, its drawn pixel content — so the browser can skip Layout and Paint entirely and just recompute things at Composite time, on a separate thread from the main JS thread. That's exactly why those two properties are the go-to choice for animations, instead of animating something like left or width."

