## Super Quick Revision (Last-Minute Read)

- **What it is**: Style → Layout → Paint → Composite — the 4 steps that turn code into pixels
- **Why staged**: so cheap changes don't pay for expensive work they don't need
- **The problem**: reading a layout value right after a layout-changing write forces the browser to recalculate immediately — do it in a loop, and it happens over and over
- **The fix**: read everything first, then write everything after (batch them separately)
- **Why transform/opacity are fast**: neither changes real layout geometry or pixel content — both can be handled entirely at the Composite stage, on a separate thread
- **Use it for**: any animation — always prefer transform/opacity over top/left/width/height
- **Remember**: `will-change` isn't free — it costs GPU memory, use it deliberately

## The One-Line Memory Trick

> **"Read, then write — never mix them."**
> For animations: **"Move the photo, don't redraw it."** `transform`/`opacity` just slide or fade an already-drawn picture (cheap, Composite-only). `left`/`width` force the browser to redraw the picture from scratch, every single frame (expensive, full pipeline).
